  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 213 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The Bermuda Ridge across the western Atlantic continues \nto strengthen and has shifted northward slightly overnight. This is \ncausing the upper level trough and associated surface reflection to \nmove slowly across the Northeast. Much of the region has been in a \nlull for precipitation over the past few hours but increasing upper \nlevel diffluence and moisture advection will soon point the fire hose \nacross portions of northern New York and Vermont. Given the lack of \nrainfall thus far, we are basically starting from scratch with soils \nthat can absorb a decent amount of rainfall. An airmass with PWAT \nvalues between 1.9-2.1 inches (99th percentile) will move overhead \nsoon and will be supportive of very efficient rain processes with \nthe freezing level between 13kft and 14kft. Rainfall rates could \nreach an inch per hour in some of the embedded convective elements \nbut rainfall rates overall should remain well under a half inch per \nhour. Still, with multiple rounds of showers possible through the \nday, the threat for flash flooding does exist albeit on the very low \nside based on how things have played out thus far.\n\nThis afternoon is a bit of a wild card as we will have the potential \nfor a few strong to locally severe thunderstorms across Vermont. \nThis potential is very conditional and is dependent on us being able \nto see a few breaks in the clouds behind the initial round (about to \ncome through now) and the frontal passage this afternoon. Lapse \nrates will generally be moist adiabatic as a result of the \nanomalously high PWAT values but any surface heating could allow for \nmore dry adiabatic lapse rates in the low levels that could mix down \nsome 40-50 knot winds down to the surface. These storms are not \nfigured to be widespread although some of the latest high-res \nguidance does show a distinct line of convection associated with the \ncold front. Basically, it'll be a messy day with several possible \nhazards so it'll be a good day to keep an eye on the weather. These \nshowers and thunderstorms will shift eastward after sunset with high \npressure quickly building into the region tonight into Tuesday \nmorning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Surface high pressure will settle into the region on \nTuesday while the Bermuda high continues to build north and west. \nThis will set the stage for deep layer ridging and bring sunny skies \nand dry weather to the region on Tuesday and Wednesday. A building \ntrough across the central US will displace the Bermuda High a bit to \nthe south on Thursday and will open up the stream of deep Gulf \nmoisture back to the region. With upper level diffluence in place, \nsome decent mid-level forcing will coincide with the moisture to \nbring scattered to numerous showers to the region Thursday afternoon \nwith the potential for a few strong thunderstorms.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: No significant changes have been made to the forecast \nfor the end of the week into the weekend, with warm and unsettled \nweather expected as the region remains in between a Bermuda high to \nour east and troughiness across the Plains. Broad southwesterly flow \nacross the region will allow for plentiful moisture to advect into \nto the region, with occasional shortwaves passing overhead helping \nto drive shower chances. As usual in this time frame, there is some \nuncertainty in to the overall extent and timing of precipitation, \nbut several rounds of showers and possible thunderstorms look likely \nat this point. With fairly weak forcing and limited instability, \nsevere weather looks unlikely however areas of locally heavy rain \nwill be possible with PWAT values near 2 inches, although there \nshould be enough flow to limit any flood threat but trends will need \nto be monitored as we get closer. Temperatures during this time \nframe look to be on the warmer side, with highs climbing into the \n80s areawide this weekend, with increased humidity making it feel a \nlittle uncomfortable at times.",
